{"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"e0a348ae","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.005861,"end_time":"2026-11-27T23:17:12.453491+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-27T23:17:12.44763+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# ASL sign generation — 1. Data preparation\n","\n","**Project.** The user says an English word in front of a camera; an avatar of the user signs that word in American Sign Language (ASL). Two notebooks train the model that turns a word into the motion of its sign:\n","\n","| Notebook | Content | Kaggle hardware |\n","|---|---|---|\n","| **1. Data preparation (this one)** | read the landmark files, explore them, clean them, split them by signer, save the dataset | CPU |\n","| 2. Model training | data for the models, metrics and sign classifier (judge), diffusion generator, final evaluation, export for the avatar | 2 × T4 GPU |\n","\n","**Data.** The Kaggle competition *Google – Isolated Sign Language Recognition* (`asl-signs`): about 94,000 clips of 250 words by 21 signers. There is no video: each clip is a `.parquet` file of MediaPipe landmarks (x, y, z of 543 points per frame).\n","\n","**Output.** The folder `asl_clean/` of the notebook output: the cleaned clips (64 frames each), one row of description per clip (word, signer, split) and `layout.json` (the meaning of each point). Notebook 2 reads it.\n","\n","| Section | Content |\n","|---|---|\n","| 1 | Open and read the parquet files |\n","| 2 | Explore the whole dataset (size, words, signers, duration, detection quality, dominant hand) |\n","| 3 | Clean the data, then compare **before / after** |\n","| 4 | Split train / validation / test **by signer** |\n","| 5 | Save the dataset for notebook 2 |\n","\n","**How to run it on Kaggle.** *Add Input → Competitions → asl-signs*, then *Save Version → Save & Run All* (a CPU session is enough). The environment variable `ASL_QUICK_TEST=1` uses 3,000 clips only, to check the notebook quickly. Keep the notebook and its output **private**: the competition rules do not allow publishing the data. Your own videos can be converted to the same format with `tools/video_to_parquet.py` of the project."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"f31cc221","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.004891,"end_time":"2026-11-27T23:17:12.463779+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-27T23:17:12.458888+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## 1. Open and read the parquet files\n","\n","The dataset contains:\n","* `train.csv`: one row per clip (file path, signer, word);\n","* `train_landmark_files/<signer>/<clip>.parquet`: the landmarks of one clip.\n","\n","A parquet file is a \"long\" table: **one row per landmark and per frame**. We first open a few of them to understand the format before processing the whole dataset."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":1,"id":"0d8dd0b9","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-27T23:17:12.475784Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-27T23:17:12.475303Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-27T23:17:13.577394Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-11-27T23:17:13.576525Z"},"papermill":{"duration":1.110006,"end_time":"2026-11-27T23:17:13.57921+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-11-27T23:17:12.469204+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["# Libraries to read and explore the data\n","import json\n","import os\n","import shutil\n","import warnings\n","from pathlib import Path\n","\n","import numpy as np\n","import pandas as pd\n","import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n","import pyarrow.parquet as pq\n","from IPython.display import display\n","from tqdm.auto import tqdm\n","\n","QUICK_TEST = os.environ.get(\"ASL_QUICK_TEST\", \"0\") == \"1\"   # True = 3,000 clips only, to test the notebook quickly\n","SEED = 42"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":2,"id":"b4bcee28","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-11-27T23:17:13.591211Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-11-27T23:17:13.590638Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-11-27T23:17:13.600622Z